{
  "Study.Name": "Mediterranean Diet Intervention for Cardiovascular Outcomes in Type 2 Diabetes (MED-CVD)",
  "authors": "Maria Rodriguez, MD, PhD; James Chen, MD, MPH; Sarah Johnson, RD, PhD; Robert Williams, MD; Lisa Park, MD, MPH",
  "funding": "National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI) Grant R01HL145678; National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK) Grant R01DK098765",
  "abstract": "This multicenter randomized controlled trial examined the effect of a structured Mediterranean diet intervention versus standard care on cardiovascular events in 1,200 adults aged 50-75 with Type 2 diabetes across 12 outpatient clinical centers. Participants were randomized 1:1 to receive intensive Mediterranean diet counseling and meal provision versus standard diabetes education. The primary outcome was a composite of myocardial infarction, stroke, and cardiovascular death over 3 years of follow-up. The study demonstrated a 25% relative risk reduction in the primary composite endpoint in the intervention group compared to standard care (adjusted for baseline HbA1c, BMI, smoking status, and statin use). Secondary outcomes included individual cardiovascular events, HbA1c changes, and dietary adherence measured at 6-month intervals.",
